cartridge "BAGGER/GAZETTE/1988"

' BAGGER
' A ColecoVision game based on "Bagger", COMPUTE!'s Gazette July 1988
' (issue 61, page 36; Commodore 64 machine language, BAGGER on the
' magazine's disk, started with SYS 2075). The program and the disk do not
' name the author. Made for Amy Studio by Claude (an AI made by Anthropic),
' 2026, in Amy by Amy Bienvenu.
'
' Not a byte-for-byte port: the rules are the original's (read from its
' machine code, orig/bagger.dis, and watched in jsc64), the game is built
' for the ColecoVision: a supermarket with four checkout belts, the belts'
' ridges running, shoppers in three kinds, brown paper bags, a bagger in
' a cap and apron, a banknote tip, sound effects and a title tune.
'
' The game: you bag the groceries at the end of four checkout belts.
' Shoppers come in from the left along the aisles behind the belts and
' walk towards you. Throw a bag down the belt: a bag that meets a shopper
' serves them (5 points). A shopper who reaches your end unserved, or a
' bag that slides off the far end of a belt, costs a life. A shopper
' well into the store (from X 50) may leave a tip on the floor that rolls
' towards your end, twice as fast: be in that aisle when it arrives (20
' points) or it is lost, and so is a life.
'
' From the original (orig/): the pass of the main loop (every bag one
' pixel left, every shopper one pixel right, every tip two pixels right,
' all on a 255-pixel belt; the bagger at the far end); the stick read
' every third pass (up and down change aisle with wrap-around, the button
' raises a bag and the next read throws it, into the first free of three
' bag places on that belt, at X 255); a bag at X 18 falls off; a shopper
' at X 255 walks out; a tip at X 255 is lost, one at X 245 or more in the
' bagger's aisle is caught; bags and shoppers meet when their X are equal
' (checked before and after the shoppers move); a new shopper in aisle
' 1-4 when a random byte is 1-4 (4 in 256 each pass), at X 1 in the first
' free of three places; a new tip the same way, from the first shopper
' at X 50 or more in an aisle with no tip; levels of 10, 30 and 50
' shoppers, then 10 again a step faster (the C64's delay 5, 4, 3, 2
' units, here passes a frame, measured in jsc64: 25 passes a second at
' delay 5); +100 a level, a life at level 5, four lives; after a lost
' life the store is cleared, the served count stays; the pauses between
' lives and levels as long as the C64's (its delay units: 11 ms).
'
' Liberties: the store is drawn 3/4 wide (X 0-255 to 16-207) and the
' rules count in the C64's pixels; the stick's left and right are
' ignored (the C64 compared the whole port, so any extra direction
' stopped a move or throw: here up, down or the button alone still count,
' and a diagonal does nothing, as there); a sound for each shopper served
' (the C64 was silent there); the passes per frame are averaged (the C64
' waited every other pass); random numbers from a 16-bit xorshift (the
' C64 used BASIC's RND); the lost bag tips off the belt, a lost shopper or
' tip blinks (3 times a second) during the pause; GAME OVER then the
' title (the C64 started a new game at once); no SHIFT pause and no RUN/
' STOP restart (no keyboard); the score is five digits and wraps at 65535
' as the C64's did; the shopper's kind (three looks) is chosen at random.
'
' Controls: controller 1 (or 2): up and down change aisle, a button
' throws a bag. On the title, up and down choose START, HOW TO PLAY
' (three pages) or CREDITS, a button chooses.
'
' House rules: text in columns 2-29, rows 1-22; tiles from code 128; the
' frame loop is protected from the NMI; screens drawn with the NMI off
' after a wait, display off. Music first in the sound table, effects after;
' the title tune is played once. `set sprite count` is always given.

const TFl0 = 128
const TFl1 = 129
const TShelf0 = 130
const TShelf1 = 131
const TBelt = 132
